+# Council Brief: Municipal Food-Waste Diversion
+
+## Decision
+
+The council is deciding whether to require food-waste separation and, if so, how to pair that requirement with collection, edible-food rescue, technical assistance, and measurement. The evidence supports a phased package rather than a stand-alone disposal ban. California supplies a broad statewide model, Seattle shows a universal local service-and-enforcement model, and Massachusetts demonstrates a generator-threshold approach. Their rules establish workable policy components, but none supplies a ready-made estimate of this city’s costs, capacity, participation, or diversion.
+
+## Sourced Findings
+
+### California
+
+California’s SB 1383 framework set 2025 statewide targets of a **75% reduction** in organic-waste disposal from the 2014 level and recovery of **20%** of edible food otherwise disposed. CalRecycle describes local implementation as organics collection combined with procurement, enforcement, planning, and edible-food recovery obligations ([CalRecycle, SB 1383 overview](https://calrecycle.ca.gov/organics/slcp/)). The 20% figure is a statewide target, not an individual generator quota; covered commercial generators must arrange to donate the maximum amount of edible food otherwise discarded, while jurisdictions operate recovery programs ([CalRecycle food-recovery FAQ](https://calrecycle.ca.gov/organics/slcp/faq/foodrecovery/)). This distinction separates rescue of edible food from processing of inedible scraps.
+
+### Seattle, Washington
+
+Seattle prohibits residents and businesses from placing food scraps and compostable paper in garbage. The city began curbside food-waste collection in 2005, required residential subscription or backyard composting in 2009, required multifamily service in 2011, and prohibited food waste from garbage in 2015. Commercial accounts can receive a fee when prohibited material exceeds **10%** of a garbage container by volume; multifamily properties can receive a **$50** charge after two warnings ([Seattle Public Utilities food-waste requirements](https://www.seattle.gov/utilities/your-services/collection-and-disposal/food-and-yard/food-waste-requirements)). Published 2026 commercial compost rates vary by container and frequency, illustrating that service design and price signals accompany the mandate ([Seattle commercial compost rates](https://www.seattle.gov/utilities/your-services/accounts-and-payments/rates/collection-and-disposal/food-and-yard-rates/commercial-compost-rates)).
+
+### Massachusetts
+
+Massachusetts uses a narrower commercial disposal ban. MassDEP’s 2014 rule covered businesses and institutions disposing at least one ton of commercial organic material weekly; effective November 1, 2022, the threshold fell to **one-half ton per week** ([MassDEP disposal-ban guide](https://www.mass.gov/guides/commercial-food-material-disposal-ban)). Compliance routes include donation, animal feed, composting, and anaerobic digestion. A 2025 MassDEP economic analysis records the threshold change and examines industry development and economic effects rather than treating collection alone as the outcome ([MassDEP 2025 economic analysis](https://www.mass.gov/doc/2025-commercial-organic-waste-ban-economic-report/download)). The model concentrates legal duties on larger generators while leaving households outside this particular ban.
+
+### Cross-Jurisdiction Lessons
+
+The cases differ in coverage, institutional responsibility, and enforcement: California joins statewide targets to local programs and food rescue; Seattle links nearly universal separation to established collection and escalating warnings; Massachusetts targets higher-volume commercial generators. EPA’s Wasted Food Scale places prevention, donation, and upcycling above composting and identifies landfill and incineration among the least preferred pathways ([EPA Wasted Food Scale](https://www.epa.gov/sustainable-management-food/wasted-food-scale)). **Inference:** a local ordinance is more transferable when it combines the hierarchy with dependable service and assistance; the observed mandates alone do not establish results for a city with different housing, hauling, or processing conditions.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+Staff recommends one phased package covering households, multifamily properties, and food businesses while distinguishing edible-food rescue from scrap processing.
+
+**Preparation:** characterize the waste stream; map collection, transfer, processing, and food-rescue capacity; consult affected renters, property managers, workers, haulers, processors, and donation partners; then set accepted materials and reporting definitions. **Launch:** provide containers, multilingual education, contamination feedback, and technical assistance before escalating enforcement; begin with large generators and city-served properties, then expand on a published schedule. **Evaluation:** review results at fixed intervals and adjust service design or enforcement using four public indicators: service **access or participation**, **contamination** rate, **tons diverted**, and **edible-food recovery**. Prevention and safe donation remain ahead of composting in program communications.
+
+## Equity and Implementation
+
+Convenient indoor and outdoor containers matter for **multifamily renters**, who often do not control building service. Property-owner duties need tenant-facing instructions and a way to report missing access without penalizing residents for building failures. **Small food businesses** need right-sized collection, start-up assistance, and training that does not assume dedicated sustainability staff. **Multilingual** materials, accessible formats, and compensated community outreach improve language access.
+
+Operationally, contracts need reliable **collection** routes, contamination protocols, and contingency terms if **processing capacity** is interrupted. Food-rescue partners need early coordination on storage, food safety, pickup schedules, and which edible products they can accept. Enforcement data need review by property type and neighborhood to detect unequal service or fee burdens.
+
+## Uncertainties and Evidence Gaps
+
+- Local residential and commercial **tonnage** and composition are unknown; a waste characterization study is needed before forecasting diversion.
+- Available transfer, **processing capacity**, route density, and backup outlets are uncertain and need market verification.
+- Local collection, container, outreach, enforcement, and food-rescue **cost** estimates are a gap; other jurisdictions’ rates are not local bids.
+- Baseline service access, **participation**, contamination, donation capacity, and impacts by housing type are unknown and need disaggregated measurement.
+
+These gaps limit any claim about local savings, emissions reductions, or compliance rates. The cited rules establish design options, not guaranteed local outcomes.
